Context (cote 140-3, 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'): Grothendieck works with an abstract profinite group G (gothic) carrying distinguished elements rho, sigma, tau' (modelled on loops around 0, 1, infinity in pi_1 of P^1 minus 3 points). G^+ is presumably an extension of G (by an involution or Z/2), and L_rho, L_sigma are the procyclic 'loop' subgroups generated by rho and sigma. Attached to these data he has built several objects:
- Sigma_{rho,sigma}: sets of solutions of the 'loop equation' [alpha,rho] = [beta,sigma] eps_1^{mu-1}, with an 'ét' subset;
- Z_{rho,sigma}: a group of automorphisms u of G^+ that send L_rho and L_sigma to G^+-conjugates of themselves, with characters gamma (cyclotomic-like), eps_Z and eps'_Z;
- Gamma_{rho,sigma}, G_{rho,sigma}, normalisers N_rho, N_sigma and N*, and the torsor-like objects S_0 Gamma and S_0 M.
The previous page gave isomorphisms identifying S_0 Gamma with S G^ff (84), Gamma with L_0 \ S G^ff (86), and S_0 M with S G^ff x S^+ (87).

Section 7, 'Fonctorialités', asks how this whole package behaves under a surjection phi: G -> G' of profinite groups, with rho', sigma', tau' taken as the images of rho, sigma, tau'. The Proposition lists equivalent conditions on phi:
(a) the induced map on loop-equation solutions Sigma^G -> Sigma^{G'} sends ét-solutions to ét-solutions;
(b) every automorphism u of G^+ normalising L_sigma, with u(L_rho) conjugate to L_rho and u(L_sigma) conjugate to L_sigma, descends to some u' in Aut(G'^+) making square (90) commute. Equivalently, u stabilises Ker phi;
(b') the same, assuming only that u(L_sigma) is conjugate to L_sigma rather than that u normalises it;
(c) there is a group homomorphism phi_Z: Z^G -> Z^{G'} compatible with gamma, eps_Z and eps'_Z, such that each u and its image satisfy (90). Such a phi_Z then induces Gamma^G -> Gamma^{G'} (marginal addition) and is compatible with i_{L_sigma,Z};
(c') the analogous statement for phi_G: G^G_{rho,sigma} -> G^{G'}_{rho,sigma}, which is unique.
The next page calls such phi 'prolongeable'. It extends phi to the normalisers N_rho, N_sigma and to S_0 M, compatibly with everything. It notes the conditions hold automatically when Sigma^{G'} equals the image of Sigma^G (e.g. for characteristic quotients). It then sketches a relative variant with a subgroup Z^natural of Z.

In modern terms: this is the descent of the GT-type ('loop equation') symmetry from a profinite group to a quotient. Descent holds exactly when Ker phi is stable under the relevant group of 'braid-like' outer automorphisms. It is the axiomatic analogue of passing from GT-hat to its actions on characteristic quotients (pro-l, pro-nilpotent, finite quotients).
